# 15:00 IST, twelve minutes before Zerodha's earliest MIS cutoff at 15:12.
#
#   sudo systemctl enable --now atlas-mis-squareoff.timer
#
# THIS TIME IS DERIVED, NOT CHOSEN HERE. atlas/config.py holds the segment
# cutoffs (verified 2026-10-09), the margin, and MIS_EXIT_TIME computed from
# them; tests/test_squareoff_timing.py asserts that the OnCalendar line below
# still equals MIS_EXIT_TIME. Change the config, then change this line, and the
# test will tell you if you changed only one.
#
# WHY IT MOVED FROM 15:15. The cutoff is an ORDER cutoff, not just a liquidation
# time: Zerodha rejects MIS orders after it. 15:15 was three minutes PAST the
# 15:12 cutoff that applies to every F&O-segment (CAS) stock, so this timer had
# never been able to close a position in that universe -- which on 2026-10-07 is
# exactly what it failed to do, with "Intraday orders (MIS) are allowed only till
# 3:12 PM".
#
# ITS OWN TIMER, NOT PART OF THE MARKET-HOURS LOOP. A crashed loop must not be
# able to strand an intraday short into broker liquidation. This is the one job
# that has to happen even when the process that opened the position is dead, so
# it fires independently of it.
#
# Mon-Fri; the runner checks the NSE calendar and exits quietly on a holiday.
#
# Persistent=FALSE, deliberately, and this is the one timer where that is right.
# A missed 15:00 cannot be usefully made up: past 15:12 an MIS exit order is
# rejected, so running late would place an order that cannot fill for a position
# the broker is already liquidating. Every other ATLAS timer sets Persistent=true
# because a late report beats none; a late square-off is not a square-off.
#
# Needs systemd 252+ for the timezone suffix; the box is 259.

[Unit]
Description=Close ATLAS MIS positions at 15:00 IST, before the 15:12 broker cutoff
Requires=atlas-mis-squareoff.service

[Timer]
OnCalendar=Mon-Fri 15:00 Asia/Kolkata
AccuracySec=10s
Persistent=false
Unit=atlas-mis-squareoff.service

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
